.4M vs $100K) and incumbency", "Expert ratings split between Safe R and Likely R, none competitive", "No public general-election polling; Polymarket prices Foxx at ~84%"], "rationale": "The district's R+9 lean, Foxx's overwhelming financial edge, and a unanimous expert consensus of Safe or Likely R leave little room for a Democratic upset, though some raters stop short of Safe R. The forecast sits modestly beyond the fundamentals-based baseline, consistent with the ratings consensus and market pricing.", "watch": "Any late polling showing Hubbard within single digits, or a major national Democratic wave environment, would be the clearest sign the race is tightening.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Virginia Foxx (R) is running."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's 5th District leans Republican by about nine points, and incumbent Virginia Foxx is seeking another term with a commanding financial position: roughly $3.4 million on hand against about $100,000 for Democratic nominee Chuck Hubbard. No outside groups are spending to help Foxx, though a small amount has been spent opposing her.\n\nNo public general-election polls are available, so the outlook rests on district fundamentals and expert assessments. All ten ratings examined place the race at Likely R or better, with most calling it Safe R, and prediction markets give Foxx roughly an 84% chance of winning. Hubbard won his primary in March but has not shown the resources or polling evidence to put the seat in play. Foxx remains the clear favorite, with a Democratic upset possible only in a strong national wave.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0624, "margin": -9.74}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0488, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0402, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.028, "margin": -13.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0498, "margin": -11.0}, {"date"